[01:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'distill it down into something where this is, you know, 10-minute film school. We\'re going to go to film school together for 10 minutes, try to figure out what What are the real tactical and practical ways that we can take a story and put it to work so that we can uh'

[01:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have our own timeless moments, our own like unforgettable stories. Okay, so we\'re going to start with some theory stuff. Let\'s go all the way back 2,300 years ago to our dear friend Aristotle.'

[01:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Here\'s how he mapped it out, you know, the stasis, the peripeteia, the pathos, then the anagnorisis, the catharsis. Sure, in English this time, normal life, there\'s a problem, there\'s a struggle, there\'s got to be a different way, and'

[01:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then finally a transformation. And then you go a little bit further to Shakespeare\'s time, what\'s wild is that same five-act structure shows up in all of Shakespeare\'s plays. There\'s this like moment that just'

[02:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'matches exac- almost exactly what what Aristotle was going after. All right, so then how about Hollywood? How about does, you know, oh my goodness, Hollywood still has that same five-part'

[02:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'structure, but we call it the three-act structure because that middle bit, that middle bit, kind of takes up uh you know, is is three little units in one, right? So, we\'ve got act one, we\'ve got act two, and we\'ve got act'

[02:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'three, and that middle bit itself is its own little journey, right? It\'s own little journey. Now, if you look at this, that\'s just an awful lot of structure, it\'s an awful lot of things to keep in your head, particularly if you\'re doing a short short short film,'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right? If you\'re doing something that\'s like 30 seconds or a minute or a few minutes long. It\'s hard to think about how am I going to force my way into this structure and get all the bits and pieces that I need in there. So, here it'

[03:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is. Here\'s the key that you can use to kind of unlock your own story and unlock your audience, which is this whole version from Aristotle to uh Shakespeare to Hollywood and beyond'

[03:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comes down to three words that rhyme. Pain, strain, and gain. Pain, strain, and gain. There\'s some sort of pain in the world, something'

[03:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'happens, there\'s strain as you struggle mightily to kind of understand it and and deal with it, and then when you finally are on the other side, when you finally get to the other end, that\'s where you gain. And this is the story of'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'transformation. This is this arc of transformation that happens at every level in a film. And it\'s almost fractal. It happens at the course of the whole film. It happens over the course of a'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sequence. And it almost always happens over the course of an individual scene, right? Because if you there is no transformation in the scene, you think to yourself, "Well, why did why did we even bother to have this?" Let\'s stick with Shrek for a'

[04:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'second. There are moments with Shrek where it was clear that some of the scenes were better than others. And it wasn\'t because they were funnier, although I like I wanted it to because because of funny cuz I came in and I had'

[04:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the honor of writing some punch-up jokes in there. So, I really wanted it to be about the humor. It wasn\'t because they were funnier or like smarter complications of plot or like, you know, better animation or cinematography, although all those things were always'

[04:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'great. It was because there was a clear emotional delta. So, Shrek walks in in one emotion. He could be happy, he could be sad, he could be anything. If he walks in in one emotion and walks out'

[04:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the same emotion, then the scene always feels kind of ho-hum. Like, well, why even have it? If there\'s no emotional change, then then what are we even doing? But if he walks in in one emotion, something happens,'

[04:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and then because of that thing, we\'re in a different state, well, then the the the scene has meaning. It has purpose. We understand why we\'re there and it feels important, right? So, if you'

[05:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if if you have a dev background, you can think of this as a state machine. A scene is an emotional state machine where you\'ve got to go from one state to another. You got to transform state from one state to another or there\'s no point really in having that scene. Then you\'re'

[05:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just doing stuff and doing stuff and doing stuff versus creating a change. All right. So, it let\'s go back for a second to those changes. Why are we making this arc of transformation? How that thing how\'s that going to help you'

[05:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'create some sort of film that\'s worth seeing and and that your friends will love. And why why is that? Well, let\'s go back to pains, trains, and gains. Let\'s think about that just for a'

[05:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'moment. Pain, strain, and gain. So, check this out. As humans, you know, we\'re we\'re we\'re kind of pathetic, you know, we lack the teeth of the tiger, we lack the'

[05:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'talons of the raptor, they\'re like hard shell of a tortoise, you know, basically we are we are nearly nothing. We are basically just like sacks of like water and meat, you know, like we don\'t'

[06:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even have sharp like fingernails, like we can\'t defend ourselves. We\'re not even the strongest like primate. But the one thing that we do have is story. We have the ability to watch what other people are doing, learn from it, and repeat it back. So, if someone gets'

[06:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into trouble somewhere and they\'re doing something, we have a way to report back, "Oh, hey, don\'t do that." Or something good happens, "Oh, wow, there\'s an amazing like pile of there\'s an orchard of apple trees over there." We have a way to report that back. So, we can escape that kind of terrible like linear'

[06:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'path of only being able to learn what you yourself can learn. We can learn from others by giving and receiving story. You know, it\'s why open source software like has like expanded like why GitHub'

[06:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'makes it so that you don\'t have to code from scratch every single time, but you can fork each others repos and get somewhere. You can build off of each others tools. But with story, we\'re building off of other human knowledge so that we can'

[06:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'create, you know, we can learn from every human who\'s ever come before us. So, why does pain, strain, and gain feel so good? It\'s because pain, strain, and gain isn\'t just like a cool technique to do to have emotional transformation for'

[07:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a story, it is indeed the exact pattern match of going from how you understood the world before to like encountering new information that is counter to your understanding of how the world worked, and then'

[07:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'incorporating that into your life by like struggling through what all the ramifications of that means, I like figuring out, "Oh, that means if this is true, then this other thing is true." Until finally finally you\'ve given up the old self, the old understanding of the world to arrive somewhere new. And'

[07:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that new place is that transformed self that is able to like do new things with this new knowledge. So story feels good to us, feels satisfying to us when it mirrors how the human mind learns.'

[07:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Okay, so if you\'re to take one thing away from me today, it\'s that story is an emotional state machine. If you want to know what process to do, remember three words that'

[08:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rhyme, pain, strain, and gain. And if you\'re at working at the individual level to try to figure out, gosh, what can I do to make my character accomplish everything I\'m trying to do,'

[08:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then here\'s a great test. Your true character is revealed by the choices they make when it really matters. When it really matters. So if you\'ve got'

[08:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'characters that are just doing stuff, no one cares. If you\'ve got characters that you put them into difficult situations and they\'ve got to make a choice, that choice is going to reveal who they are'

[08:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and what they, you know, who they are at their core and and what they really care about. Okay, so that\'s a bit of um 10 minutes of film school. Uh we\'re here to create characters who uh reveal'

[08:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'their true choices by the I\'m their true character by the choices they make when it really matters. We\'re creating emotional state machines. We\'re following the structure of pain, strain,'

[09:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
